  "title": "7 Kubernetes resource traps that bite in prod",
  "content": "After enough code that touches designing humanintheloop gets reviewed, the same failure pattern keeps showing up. This walks through the fix and the reasoning, not just the patch. The workaround gets copy-pasted forward long after the original reason is forgotten.\n\nKubernetes resource requests and limits look like two simple numbers per container. In practice they encode scheduling, eviction, CPU throttling, memory OOM kills, and the difference between a rollout that finishes and one that wedges. The failure mode is rarely a crash on apply. It's a pod that schedules onto a node with no headroom, a Java process that gets throttled to a crawl because its limit is half a core, or a node that starts evicting your payment service because a batch job's request was set to the default. This post covers seven traps that show up when teams move from \"it works on my laptop\" to \"it works under load in Lagos on a 3G connection\" — and what to do about each.\n\nThe concrete problem this post solves: teams set requests and limits once, never revisit them, and then spend days debugging p99 latency and OOMKilled pods that trace back to a single mis-sized number in a Deployment manifest.\n\n## Why requests and limits are two different contracts\n\nBefore the list, the mental model. A **request** is what the scheduler uses to decide which node has room. It is a promise to the kubelet that this container will get at least that much. A **limit** is what the kernel enforces at runtime: exceed the CPU limit and you get throttled; exceed the memory limit and you get OOM-killed.\n\nThey are not symmetric. CPU is compressible — throttling slows you down but doesn't kill you. Memory is incompressible — going over the limit terminates the process. That asymmetry drives most of the traps below.\n\nIf you set a request but no limit, the container can burst to use idle CPU on the node, and memory is unbounded until the node itself runs out and the eviction manager starts killing pods by QoS class. If you set a limit but no request, the scheduler assumes zero and will happily pack your pod onto a node that's already full — then throttle it to the limit the moment it tries to work.\n\nThe Kubernetes docs on [requests and limits](https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/configuration/manage-resources-containers/) are the canonical reference; read them once, then come back to the traps.\n\n## The seven traps\n\n### 1. The CPU limit that turns a fast service into a slow one\n\n**What it does:** A CPU limit caps how many CPU-seconds a container can consume per period (typically 100ms on the CFS quota). Hit the cap and the kernel throttles the container until the next period.\n\n**Strength:** Prevents a runaway process — a hot loop, a badly tuned GC — from starving every other pod on the node.\n\n**Weakness:** Throttling is invisible in most dashboards unless you're watching `container_cpu_cfs_throttled_seconds_total`. A service with a 500m limit and a bursty request pattern will show p99 latency spikes that look like network problems but are actually CFS quota exhaustion. Go and Java services with large thread pools are especially prone because they wake many threads at once.\n\n**Best for:** Noisy-neighbour protection on shared nodes where you genuinely cannot trust the workload. For latency-sensitive services, many teams drop the CPU limit entirely and rely on requests plus node autoscaling. That's a defensible default — but only if you've set requests high enough that the scheduler doesn't overpack nodes.\n\n**Check:** Query `rate(container_cpu_cfs_throttled_seconds_total[5m])` per pod. If it's non-zero for a service that isn't supposed to be CPU-bound, your limit is too tight or your request is too low.\n\n### 2. The memory limit set from a laptop observation\n\n**What it does:** Memory limits are enforced by the cgroup OOM killer. Exceed them and the kernel sends SIGKILL to the container's main process. Kubernetes reports `OOMKilled` in the pod status.\n\n**Strength:** Hard ceiling. One leaking pod can't take down the node.\n\n**Weakness:** Peak memory in a container is not the same as steady-state memory. A Go service that holds a large in-memory cache, a JVM with a heap sized as a fraction of the limit, or a Python process that spikes during a batch import will all exceed a limit sized from a quiet-period observation. Worse, the JVM historically reads the cgroup limit to size its default heap — but only if the runtime is new enough to be cgroup-aware. Older JVMs size the heap from the node's total memory, blow past the container limit, and get OOM-killed on startup.\n\n**Best for:** Every container. But size the limit from an observed peak under realistic load, not from an idle `kubectl top`. Give yourself headroom — a limit equal to your observed peak is a limit you will hit.\n\n**Check:** `kubectl describe pod <name>` and look for `Last State: Terminated, Reason: OOMKilled`. If it's there, the limit is the problem, not the node.\n\n### 3. Requests so low the scheduler overpacks the node\n\n**What it does:** The scheduler sums requests across all pods on a node and refuses to place a pod if the sum would exceed allocatable capacity. Requests are the scheduler's only signal.\n\n**Strength:** Predictable placement. If every pod declares honest requests, no node is oversubscribed and the eviction manager has little to do.\n\n**Weakness:** Teams often set requests to a token value — 10m CPU, 32Mi memory — because \"it schedules fine.\" It does schedule fine. Then the node fills with pods that each want ten times their request, the kubelet's eviction manager starts ranking pods by QoS class, and your BestEffort batch job gets killed while the Guaranteed database pod survives. The batch job's owner sees a mysterious eviction and blames the platform team.\n\n**Best for:** All workloads. The request should approximate what the container needs to run well, not the minimum that lets it start.\n\n**Check:** Compare `sum(kube_pod_container_resource_requests)` per node against `kube_node_status_allocatable`. If requests are far below actual usage, you have a scheduling debt that will surface as evictions.\n\n### 4. The QoS class nobody chose\n\n**What it does:** Kubernetes assigns each pod a QoS class based on how requests and limits are set: **Guaranteed** (requests equal limits for every container and every resource), **Burstable** (some requests set, not equal to limits), **BestEffort** (no requests or limits at all).\n\n**Strength:** QoS class determines eviction order under node pressure. Guaranteed pods are evicted last; BestEffort pods first.\n\n**Weakness:** QoS class is a side effect of how you wrote the manifest, not a field you set. Teams that set only limits get Burstable; teams that set nothing get BestEffort. A critical payment service with no requests is BestEffort and will be the first thing killed when a node runs low on memory — even though it's the most important workload on the node.\n\n**Best for:** Anything you care about should be at least Burstable with honest requests; anything you cannot afford to lose should be Guaranteed. The cost of Guaranteed is that you can't burst, so reserve it for services with predictable load.\n\n**Check:** `kubectl get pod <name> -o jsonpath='{.status.qosClass}'`. Audit your production namespace and see how many critical services are BestEffort.\n\n### 5. The LimitRange default that silently rewrites your manifest\n\n**What it does:** A `LimitRange` in a namespace injects default requests and limits into pods that don't specify them, and can enforce min/max bounds.\n\n**Strength:** Prevents the BestEffort-by-accident problem. A cluster admin can set sane defaults so a developer who forgets to size a container still gets something reasonable.\n\n**Weakness:** The default is invisible in the Deployment manifest. A developer reads the YAML, sees no resources block, and assumes the pod is unbounded. It isn't — it's whatever the LimitRange says, which might be 100m CPU and 128Mi memory. The pod gets throttled or OOM-killed and the manifest offers no clue. This is especially painful for teams migrating workloads between namespaces with different LimitRanges.\n\n**Best for:** Platform teams enforcing a floor. But document the defaults and surface them in your developer portal or a `kubectl get limitrange -o yaml` snippet in the onboarding docs.\n\n**Check:** `kubectl get limitrange -n <namespace> -o yaml`. If you don't know what's in there, you don't know what your pods are actually running with.\n\n### 6. The HPA that scales on CPU when CPU is capped\n\n**What it does:** The HorizontalPodAutoscaler scales replicas based on a metric — commonly CPU utilisation as a percentage of request.\n\n**Strength:** Automatic capacity for variable load. Works well when the metric tracks the actual bottleneck.\n\n**Weakness:** HPA compares observed CPU against the **request**, not the limit. If you set a CPU limit but a low request, the HPA sees high utilisation against the request and scales out aggressively — even though each pod is throttled at the limit and adding replicas doesn't help. Conversely, if the request is high and the limit is low, the HPA may never scale because utilisation against the request stays low while every pod is throttled. The HPA and the limit are fighting each other.\n\n**Best for:** Services where CPU is genuinely the bottleneck and requests are honest. For I/O-bound services, scale on a custom metric (queue depth, in-flight requests) instead of CPU.\n\n**Check:** `kubectl describe hpa <name>` and look at the current vs target utilisation. Cross-reference with the throttling metric from trap 1. If both are high, your request/limit ratio is wrong.\n\n### 7. The sidecar with no resources at all\n\n**What it does:** Service meshes, log shippers, and metric exporters run as sidecars or DaemonSets alongside your application container.\n\n**Strength:** They provide observability, mTLS, and traffic policy without changing application code.\n\n**Weakness:** Sidecars are containers. They consume CPU and memory. A service mesh sidecar that proxies every request can add measurable latency and memory per pod. If the sidecar has no requests, the scheduler doesn't account for it; if it has no limits, a sidecar leak can OOM the pod. Many teams size the application container carefully and leave the sidecar at defaults, then wonder why the pod's total memory footprint is higher than the sum of its parts.\n\n**Best for:** Every sidecar should have requests and limits set, even if the mesh's injection template doesn't do it by default. Check the injector configuration.\n\n**Check:** `kubectl get pod <name> -o jsonpath='{.spec.containers[*].name}'` to list containers, then inspect each one's resources. If any container has an empty resources block, that's a gap.\n\n## How to prioritise which trap to fix first\n\nNot all seven matter equally. A practical order, based on blast radius:\n\n1. **OOMKilled pods in production.** Memory limits that are too low cause hard outages. Fix these first. Look for `OOMKilled` in pod status across your production namespace.\n2. **BestEffort workloads that matter.** Any service in the critical path with no requests is one node-pressure event away from eviction. Set requests at minimum.\n3. **CPU throttling on latency-sensitive services.** This shows up as p99 latency that doesn't correlate with traffic. Check the throttling metric before you blame the network.\n4. **HPA and limit mismatch.** If autoscaling isn't helping under load, the request/limit ratio is the likely cause.\n5. **LimitRange defaults you didn't document.** Lower urgency, but it's the root cause of many \"why is this pod throttled\" tickets.\n6. **Sidecar resources.** Important for cost and stability, but usually a smaller blast radius than the application container.\n7. **Requests set too low across the board.** This is a scheduling hygiene issue that compounds over time. Fix it with a policy (admission controller or LimitRange) rather than a one-off audit.\n\nThe principle: fix what causes outages first, then what causes latency, then what causes confusion.\n\n## A minimal manifest that avoids all seven\n\nHere is a Deployment fragment that sets requests equal to limits for a latency-sensitive service (Guaranteed QoS), includes a sidecar with its own resources, and leaves room for the HPA to scale on a request that reflects reality. The numbers are illustrative — replace them with values derived from your own load tests.\n\n```yaml\napiVersion: apps/v1\nkind: Deployment\nmetadata:\n  name: payments-api\nspec:\n  replicas: 3\n  selector:\n    matchLabels:\n      app: payments-api\n  template:\n    metadata:\n      labels:\n        app: payments-api\n    spec:\n      containers:\n        - name: api\n          image: registry.example.com/payments-api:1.4.2\n          resources:\n            requests:\n              cpu: \"500m\"\n              memory: \"512Mi\"\n            limits:\n              cpu: \"500m\"\n              memory: \"512Mi\"\n          ports:\n            - containerPort: 8080\n        - name: log-shipper\n          image: registry.example.com/log-shipper:2.1.0\n          resources:\n            requests:\n              cpu: \"50m\"\n              memory: \"64Mi\"\n            limits:\n              cpu: \"100m\"\n              memory: \"128Mi\"\n```\n\nTwo things to notice. First, the application container has requests equal to limits, so the pod is Guaranteed and will be evicted last. Second, the sidecar has its own resources — it is not invisible to the scheduler. If your service is bursty and you'd rather have Burstable QoS, drop the CPU limit on the application container but keep the request honest; the memory limit should stay to prevent node-level OOM.\n\nFor a batch job that can tolerate eviction, BestEffort is sometimes acceptable — but only if you've labelled it as such and the team owning the node knows it. The trap is accidental BestEffort, not deliberate.\n\n## Frequently Asked Questions\n\n### What happens if I set a CPU limit but no CPU request?\n\nThe scheduler treats the request as zero, so it will place the pod on any node with free capacity — including one that's already heavily loaded. At runtime the container is capped at the limit, but the scheduler had no way to reserve capacity for it. The pod may be throttled immediately under load, and the node may be oversubscribed. Always set a request; the limit is optional depending on your latency tolerance.\n\n### Why does my pod get OOMKilled when memory usage looks fine in kubectl top?\n\n`kubectl top` shows a point-in-time sample, not the peak. A container can spike between samples — during a GC pause, a large request, or a batch import — and hit the cgroup limit before the next sample. The kernel kills the process immediately. Check `kubectl describe pod` for `Last State: Terminated, Reason: OOMKilled` and compare the limit against your observed peak under load, not against the average.\n\n### Should I always set requests equal to limits?\n\nNo. Equal requests and limits give you Guaranteed QoS, which means no bursting and last-priority eviction. That's right for services with predictable load that you cannot afford to lose. For services with spiky traffic, a lower request and a higher limit (Burstable) lets the pod use idle node capacity while still reserving a floor. The wrong answer is leaving both unset and getting BestEffort by accident.\n\n### How do I know if my CPU limit is causing latency?\n\nLook at the `container_cpu_cfs_throttled_seconds_total` metric for the pod. If it's increasing while your latency is spiking, the limit is the cause. A second signal is that latency improves when you remove the CPU limit but keep the request — that confirms the limit was the bottleneck, not the request.\n\n## The next 30 minutes\n\nRun this against your production namespace and look for pods with no requests or no limits:\n\n```bash\nkubectl get pods -n production -o json | \\\n  jq -r '.items[] | .metadata.name as $n | .spec.containers[] | select(.resources.requests == null or .resources.limits == null) | \"\\($n) / \\(.name)\"'\n```\n\nEvery line in the output is a container that is either BestEffort (no requests) or unbounded (no limits). Pick the one in the most critical path, check its actual usage with `kubectl top pod`, and set a request based on that number. That single change is the highest-leverage fix you can make today.\n\n\n---\n\n### About this article\n\n**Written by:** [Kubai Kevin](/about/) — software developer based in Nairobi, Kenya.\n\n**How this article was produced:** This site uses an automated LLM pipeline designed and maintained by the author. Drafts pass automated quality gates (minimum length, uniqueness, concrete metrics, versioned tools, code samples, absence of filler). Individual line-by-line human editing is not performed on every post before publication. Figures, benchmarks and scenarios are illustrative unless a source is linked; verify them against official documentation before relying on them in production. See the AI content policy.\n\n**Corrections:** Report errors via the [contact page](/contact/). Corrections are applied promptly.\n\n**Last generated:** November 2026\n",
